🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗶𝘀 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗯𝘂𝘁 𝗨𝘀𝗲𝗿𝘀 𝗖𝗮𝗻𝗻𝗼𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗰𝗵 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗔𝗽𝗽 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your pod is Running and Ready, but users are still unable to access the application. How will you troubleshoot it?”

At this point, I would stop focusing only on the pod.

I would trace the complete network path from the user to the application.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check the Service

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>

Verify the Service type, ports and target port.

2️⃣ Check Service endpoints

kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>

If there are no endpoints, I would check the Service selector and pod labels.

3️⃣ Check Ingress

kubectl get ingress -n <namespace>
kubectl describe ingress <ingress-name> -n <namespace>

Check:

🔹 Hostname
🔹 Backend service
🔹 Port
🔹 TLS configuration
🔹 Ingress controller events

4️⃣ Test DNS

nslookup <application-domain>

Verify that the domain resolves to the expected endpoint.

5️⃣ Test the application internally

From inside the cluster:

curl http://<service-name>:<port>

If the internal request works but external access fails, I would focus on the Ingress / Load Balancer / DNS / network layer.

6️⃣ Check Load Balancer and network configuration

Depending on the environment, I would check:

🔹 Load Balancer
🔹 Firewall rules
🔹 Network policies
🔹 Security groups
🔹 Ingress controller
🔹 TLS certificates

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

User → DNS → Load Balancer/Ingress → Service → Endpoint → Pod → Application

The key is to identify exactly where the request stops.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

Pod Running + Ready does not guarantee external accessibility.

Good DevOps troubleshooting means following the request path layer by layer instead of making random changes. 🚀
